    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old woman is brought to the clinic by her spouse, who reports that the patient ''turns the television up to maximum volume'' and frequently withdraws from family conversations. During the CGA, the clinician performs the **handheld whisper test** (standing 2 feet behind the patient, whispering a sequence of numbers/letters while masking the contralateral ear), and the patient fails to repeat the sequence. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the clinical significance of hearing loss in older adults?', '["Meniere''s disease; treat the patient immediately with high-dose intravenous diuretics and a fluid-restricted diet", "Acoustic neuroma (vestibular schwannoma); this patient requires an immediate referral for brain surgery", "Presbyacusis (age-related high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss); hearing loss is strongly associated with social isolation, depression, and is one of the most modifiable risk factors for accelerated cognitive decline/dementia", "Otosclerosis (conductive hearing loss); hearing loss has no correlation with cognitive or psychological function in older adults", "Normal auditory aging; tell the spouse to speak in a loud, high-pitched scream to communicate with the patient"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hearing loss affects over two-thirds of adults over age 70. The most common cause is **presbyacusis**—characterized by progressive, bilateral, symmetric, **high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss** caused by the degeneration of cochlear hair cells. The handheld whisper test is a highly sensitive and specific screening tool. In the CGA, addressing hearing loss is vital because untreated hearing impairment is a powerful **independent risk factor for social isolation, depression, and cognitive decline**. Crucially, the Lancet Commission on Dementia identified midlife/late-life hearing loss as the **single largest modifiable risk factor for dementia**, accounting for an 8% relative reduction in dementia risk if fully corrected with hearing aids. Speaking in a high-pitched voice is counterproductive because high frequencies are lost first; instead, speak in a clear, deep, low-pitched voice facing the patient. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 46; Livingston et al., Lancet 2020)'),
