f fully corrected with hearing aids. Speaking in a high-pitched voice is counterproductive because high frequencies are lost first; instead, speak in a clear, deep, low-pitched voice facing the patient. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 46; Livingston et al., Lancet 2020)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old man is brought to the clinic by his spouse, who reports that the patient ''turns the television up to maximum volume'' and frequently withdraws from family conversations. During the CGA, the clinician performs the **handheld whisper test** (standing 2 feet behind the patient, whispering a sequence of numbers/letters while masking the contralateral ear), and the patient fails to repeat the sequence. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the clinical significance of hearing loss in older adults?', '["Normal auditory aging; tell the spouse to speak in a loud, high-pitched scream to communicate with the patient", "Otosclerosis (conductive hearing loss); hearing loss has no correlation with cognitive or psychological function in older adults", "Acoustic neuroma (vestibular schwannoma); this patient requires an immediate referral for brain surgery", "Presbyacusis (age-related high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss); hearing loss is strongly associated with social isolation, depression, and is one of the most modifiable risk factors for accelerated cognitive decline/dementia", "Meniere''s disease; treat the patient immediately with high-dose intravenous diuretics and a fluid-restricted diet"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hearing loss affects over two-thirds of adults over age 70. The most common cause is **presbyacusis**—characterized by progressive, bilateral, symmetric, **high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss** caused by the degeneration of cochlear hair cells. The handheld whisper test is a highly sensitive and specific screening tool. In the CGA, addressing hearing loss is vital because untreated hearing impairment is a powerful **independent risk factor for social isolation, depression, and cognitive decline**. Crucially, the Lancet Commission on Dementia identified midlife/late-life hearing loss as the **single largest modifiable risk factor for dementia**, accounting for an 8% relative reduction in dementia risk if fully corrected with hearing aids. Speaking in a high-pitched voice is counterproductive because high frequencies are lost first; instead, speak in a clear, deep, low-pitched voice facing the patient. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 46; Livingston et al., Lancet 2020)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female with moderate knee osteoarthritis, mild peripheral neuropathy, and a history of a recent non-syncopal fall is preparing for discharge after a brief hospital stay. The team is addressing fall prevention. Which of the following represents the most evidence-based, high-yield intervention to improve home safety and reduce the risk of recurrent falls?', '["Order a commercial wheelchair and advise the patient to never attempt standing again", "Prescribe low-dose benzodiazepines nightly to reduce the patient''s fear of falling during ambulation", "Advise the patient to carpet the entire house with thick shag rugs to cushion the impact of future falls", "Refer the patient for a home safety evaluation by an Occupational Therapist (OT) to assess for environmental hazards (e.g., remove throw rugs, improve lighting, install grab bars in the bathroom, add handrails to stairs) and provide adaptive equipment", "Advise the patient to remain seated or in bed for at least 22 hours a day to prevent all physical contact with environmental hazards"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Environmental hazards in the home are responsible for 30–50% of falls in older adults. A home safety assessment performed by an **Occupational Therapist (OT)** is a highly effective, evidence-based intervention that reduces fall rates by up to 30% in high-risk patients. The OT evaluates the patient performing functional activities in their own environment and recommends: (1) **Removing hazards** — especially throw rugs, electrical cords, and clutter; (2) **Bathroom modifications** — installing professional grab bars (not towel racks) in the tub/shower and next to the toilet, using a raised toilet seat or tub transfer bench; (3) **Stair safety** — securing double handrails; (4) **Lighting** — installing nightlights in hallways and bright, glare-free lighting on stairwells. Thick carpeting, benzodiazepines (which increase falls per Beers Criteria), and physical restraints actually *increase* the risk of falls and injury. (Ref: Gillespie et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2012; Hazzard''s Ch. 24)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old female with moderate knee osteoarthritis, mild peripheral neuropathy, and a history of a recent non-syncopal fall is preparing for discharge after a brief hospital stay. The team is addressing fall prevention. Which of the following represents the most evidence-based, high-yield intervention to improve home safety and reduce the risk of recurrent falls?', '["Refer the patient for a home safety evaluation by an Occupational Therapist (OT) to assess for environmental hazards (e.g., remove throw rugs, improve lighting, install grab bars in the bathroom, add handrails to stairs) and provide adaptive equipment", "Advise the patient to carpet the entire house with thick shag rugs to cushion the impact of future falls", "Order a commercial wheelchair and advise the patient to never attempt standing again", "Prescribe low-dose benzodiazepines nightly to reduce the patient''s fear of falling during ambulation", "Advise the patient to remain seated or in bed for at least 22 hours a day to prevent all physical contact with environmental hazards"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Environmental hazards in the home are responsible for 30–50% of falls in older adults. A home safety assessment performed by an **Occupational Therapist (OT)** is a highly effective, evidence-based intervention that reduces fall rates by up to 30% in high-risk patients. The OT evaluates the patient performing functional activities in their own environment and recommends: (1) **Removing hazards** — especially throw rugs, electrical cords, and clutter; (2) **Bathroom modifications** — installing professional grab bars (not towel racks) in the tub/shower and next to the toilet, using a raised toilet seat or tub transfer bench; (3) **Stair safety** — securing double handrails; (4) **Lighting** — installing nightlights in hallways and bright, glare-free lighting on stairwells. Thick carpeting, benzodiazepines (which increase falls per Beers Criteria), and physical restraints actually *increase* the risk of falls and injury. (Ref: Gillespie et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2012; Hazzard''s Ch. 24)'),
