    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old man with moderate knee osteoarthritis, mild peripheral neuropathy, and a history of a recent non-syncopal fall is preparing for discharge after a brief hospital stay. The team is addressing fall prevention. Which of the following represents the most evidence-based, high-yield intervention to improve home safety and reduce the risk of recurrent falls?', '["Refer the patient for a home safety evaluation by an Occupational Therapist (OT) to assess for environmental hazards (e.g., remove throw rugs, improve lighting, install grab bars in the bathroom, add handrails to stairs) and provide adaptive equipment", "Order a commercial wheelchair and advise the patient to never attempt standing again", "Advise the patient to carpet the entire house with thick shag rugs to cushion the impact of future falls", "Prescribe low-dose benzodiazepines nightly to reduce the patient''s fear of falling during ambulation", "Advise the patient to remain seated or in bed for at least 22 hours a day to prevent all physical contact with environmental hazards"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Environmental hazards in the home are responsible for 30–50% of falls in older adults. A home safety assessment performed by an **Occupational Therapist (OT)** is a highly effective, evidence-based intervention that reduces fall rates by up to 30% in high-risk patients. The OT evaluates the patient performing functional activities in their own environment and recommends: (1) **Removing hazards** — especially throw rugs, electrical cords, and clutter; (2) **Bathroom modifications** — installing professional grab bars (not towel racks) in the tub/shower and next to the toilet, using a raised toilet seat or tub transfer bench; (3) **Stair safety** — securing double handrails; (4) **Lighting** — installing nightlights in hallways and bright, glare-free lighting on stairwells. Thick carpeting, benzodiazepines (which increase falls per Beers Criteria), and physical restraints actually *increase* the risk of falls and injury. (Ref: Gillespie et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2012; Hazzard''s Ch. 24)'),
