e risk of falls and injury. (Ref: Gillespie et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2012; Hazzard''s Ch. 24)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old woman with mild-to-moderate Alzheimer''s disease is brought to the clinic. The family reports that the patient has had two minor car accidents in the past month, frequently gets lost in familiar areas, and has difficulty staying in her lane. The patient insists that she is a ''perfectly safe driver.'' Which of the following is the most appropriate clinical and ethical management step?', '["Perform a comprehensive evaluation (including vision screening, cognitive testing of executive/visuospatial function, and neck range of motion), advise the patient to immediately stop driving, and refer the patient to a certified driving rehabilitation specialist (CDRS) for a formal on-road driving evaluation", "Reassure the family that because the accidents were minor, the patient can continue driving as long as she avoids driving at rush hour", "Immediately notify the police department to have the patient arrested and the vehicle impounded", "Prescribe a high dose of a cholinesterase inhibitor and inform the patient that this completely restores safe driving abilities", "Tell the family that legally the physician has no right to discuss driving safety and they must manage the issue without medical input"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Driving is a highly valued IADL that represents independence, but the safety of the patient and the public is paramount. When driving safety is questioned (due to accidents, getting lost, or cognitive decline), the clinician must: (1) **Assess key driving-related domains** — vision (acuity/visual fields), cognition (executive function, trail making, visuospatial skills), and motor skills (neck rotation, reaction time); (2) **Counsel the patient and family** — if severe deficits are present (such as in moderate-to-severe dementia), the patient must be advised to **stop driving immediately**; (3) **Refer for formal evaluation** — if the case is borderline or the patient lacks insight, refer to a **Certified Driving Rehabilitation Specialist (CDRS)** for a formal, standardized off-road and on-road driving assessment; (4) **Understand legal duties** — in many jurisdictions, physicians are mandated or permitted to report cognitively impaired drivers to the department of motor vehicles. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 73; AMA Ethical Guidelines on Impaired Drivers)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old man with mild-to-moderate Alzheimer''s disease is brought to the clinic. The family reports that the patient has had two minor car accidents in the past month, frequently gets lost in familiar areas, and has difficulty staying in his lane. The patient insists that he is a ''perfectly safe driver.'' Which of the following is the most appropriate clinical and ethical management step?', '["Prescribe a high dose of a cholinesterase inhibitor and inform the patient that this completely restores safe driving abilities", "Immediately notify the police department to have the patient arrested and the vehicle impounded", "Perform a comprehensive evaluation (including vision screening, cognitive testing of executive/visuospatial function, and neck range of motion), advise the patient to immediately stop driving, and refer the patient to a certified driving rehabilitation specialist (CDRS) for a formal on-road driving evaluation", "Tell the family that legally the physician has no right to discuss driving safety and they must manage the issue without medical input", "Reassure the family that because the accidents were minor, the patient can continue driving as long as he avoids driving at rush hour"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Driving is a highly valued IADL that represents independence, but the safety of the patient and the public is paramount. When driving safety is questioned (due to accidents, getting lost, or cognitive decline), the clinician must: (1) **Assess key driving-related domains** — vision (acuity/visual fields), cognition (executive function, trail making, visuospatial skills), and motor skills (neck rotation, reaction time); (2) **Counsel the patient and family** — if severe deficits are present (such as in moderate-to-severe dementia), the patient must be advised to **stop driving immediately**; (3) **Refer for formal evaluation** — if the case is borderline or the patient lacks insight, refer to a **Certified Driving Rehabilitation Specialist (CDRS)** for a formal, standardized off-road and on-road driving assessment; (4) **Understand legal duties** — in many jurisdictions, physicians are mandated or permitted to report cognitively impaired drivers to the department of motor vehicles. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 73; AMA Ethical Guidelines on Impaired Drivers)'),
