    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old female with moderate dementia and osteoarthritis is brought to the clinic for a routine visit. On physical examination, the nurse notes multiple linear bruises on the patient''s upper back, a deep pressure injury over the sacrum with a foul odor, and severe perineal dermatitis from soiled incontinence briefs. The patient appears quiet and looks at her primary caregiver (adult child) before answering any questions. The caregiver appears angry and complains loudly about the financial and physical burden of care. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Discontinue all pain medications and cognitive therapies to simplify the caregiver''s daily routine", "Reassure the caregiver that caregiver burden is normal and no interventions are needed since the bruising is likely due to ''easy bruising'' of aging", "Confront the caregiver aggressively in front of the patient and demand that they apologize for the poor care", "Admit the patient to the hospital and refuse to allow the caregiver to visit, without filing any external reports", "Interview the patient alone in a safe, private space to screen for elder abuse and neglect, and file a report immediately with Adult Protective Services (APS) or the state regulatory agency based on clinical suspicion of neglect and physical abuse"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Elder abuse and neglect affect up to 10% of older adults, with cognitively impaired individuals being at the highest risk. Signs of **neglect** include poor hygiene, untreated pressure ulcers, soiled clothing, and malnutrition. Signs of **physical abuse** include bruises on non-exposed areas (back, inner thighs) that are atypical for accidental falls (which usually occur on bony prominences like knees and elbows). Clinicians must: (1) **Interview the patient alone** to ask direct, non-judgmental questions; (2) **Identify caregiver burnout/stress**, which is a major risk factor for abuse; (3) **Mandatory Reporting** — in almost all states, clinicians are legally mandated to report any **reasonable suspicion** of elder abuse, neglect, or financial exploitation to **Adult Protective Services (APS)**. You do not need absolute proof; clinical suspicion is the threshold for reporting. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 14; Lachs & Pillemer, N Engl J Med 2015)'),
