    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old male with a progressive, life-limiting neurodegenerative condition is undergoing a Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment. The clinical team is facilitating **Advance Care Planning (ACP)**. The patient states, ''If my heart stops or I can''t breathe on my own, I do not want machines keeping me alive, and I want my oldest child to make decisions for me if I can''t speak.'' Which of the following represents the most legally and clinically appropriate set of documents to execute this patient''s wishes?', '["Execute a unilateral ''Do Not Resuscitate'' (DNR) order in the chart without discussing the decision with the designated surrogate", "Create a standard financial will, as this is the primary document used by physicians to determine resuscitation status in the emergency department", "Inform the patient that advance care planning is only permitted for patients who are actively dying in an ICU setting", "Execute a Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare (to designate the surrogate decision-maker), complete a Living Will (outlining preferences for life-sustaining treatments), and translate these into an actionable Physician Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment (POLST/MOLST) form signed by the clinician", "Advise the patient that oral statements to the family are the only legally binding form of advance directive in all states"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Advance Care Planning (ACP) involves two key components: (1) **Designating a surrogate decision-maker** — via a **Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare** (Healthcare Proxy); (2) **Documenting treatment preferences** — via a **Living Will**. In patients with advanced illness or progressive decline, these preferences should be translated into a **Physician Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment (POLST)** or Medical Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment (MOLST) form. Unlike a living will (which is a general planning document), a POLST/MOLST is a set of **actionable medical orders** signed by a clinician that is highly visible and must be honored by emergency medical services (EMS) across care settings (home, nursing home, hospital). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 15; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
