    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old man presents with progressive ankle swelling and a history of hypertension. The patient was started on **nifedipine** 10 mg daily by another physician 2 months ago. A week later, due to bilateral ankle edema, he was prescribed **furosemide** 40 mg daily. The patient now presents with frequent nocturnal urination, muscle cramps, and orthostatic dizziness. Which of the following clinical concepts does this scenario represent, and what is the correct management?', '["Standard multi-drug management of essential hypertension; the ankle edema indicates new-onset congestive heart failure requiring an increase in both medications", "An acute allergic reaction to furosemide; the patient should be treated with high-dose intravenous corticosteroids indefinitely", "A drug-drug interaction that can be resolved by changing the time of day the medications are taken, without altering the doses", "A genetic resistance to calcium channel blockers; the patient requires immediate referral for a renal biopsy", "A prescribing cascade, where a side effect of one medication (nifedipine-induced peripheral edema) is misdiagnosed as a new medical condition, leading to the prescription of an unnecessary and harmful second medication (furosemide); discontinue furosemide, reduce or stop nifedipine, and switch to a safer antihypertensive class (e.g., ACE inhibitor or ARB)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is a classic example of a **prescribing cascade**—a critical clinical concept in geriatrics. A prescribing cascade occurs when an **adverse drug reaction (ADR) is misinterpreted as a new medical condition**, and a subsequent drug is prescribed to treat the symptom. Calcium channel blockers (like **amlodipine** or **nifedipine**) cause arteriolar vasodilation, leading to **fluid transudation and peripheral edema** (a benign, non-cardiac, non-renal side effect). Prescribing a loop diuretic (furosemide) to treat this edema is ineffective, inappropriate, and exposes the patient to 