    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old female with a history of type 2 diabetes, chronic kidney disease (eGFR 35 mL/min), and chronic gout is evaluated. The patient''s medication list is reviewed using the **STOPP/START criteria** (Screening Tool of Older Persons'' Prescriptions and Screening Tool to Alert to Right Treatment). The patient is currently taking **glimepiride** (a long-acting sulfonylurea) and **NSAIDs** (naproxen daily for joint pain), but is NOT taking an **ACE inhibitor** or an **osteoporosis preventive** despite a history of a recent vertebral fracture. Which of the following represents the most appropriate set of interventions based on STOPP/START principles?', '["The criteria recommend discontinuing all medications and initiating full-time homeopathic remedies", "Discontinue the ACE inhibitor and double the dose of daily NSAIDs to maximize pain control", "The STOPP/START criteria are used only in pediatric medicine; no medication changes are warranted", "Continue all current medications and add high-dose prednisone daily to manage the joint pain", "Discontinue glimepiride (STOPP; high risk of prolonged hypoglycemia) and switch to a safer agent; discontinue daily NSAIDs (STOPP; high risk of worsening CKD and GI bleed); and initiate an ACE inhibitor (START; renal protection in diabetes) and a bisphosphonate (START; osteoporosis treatment)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **STOPP/START criteria** are an evidence-based clinical tool used to identify potentially inappropriate medications (**STOPP**) and potentially omitted indicated medications (**START**) in older adults. (1) **STOPP targets in this patient**: **Long-acting sulfonylureas (glimepiride, glyburide)** should be stopped due to a very high risk of severe, prolonged hypoglycemia; **NSAIDs** should be stopped in patients with chronic kidney disease (eGFR <60) due to risks of acute kidney injury, fluid retention, and gastrointestinal bleeding. (2) **START targets in this patient**: An **ACE inhibitor or ARB** is indicated for renal protection in patients with diabetes and albuminuria/CKD; a **bisphosphonate or other anti-resorptive** is strongly indicated for osteoporosis prevention in an older patient with a prior fragility fracture (vertebral fracture). (Ref: O''Mahony et al., Age Ageing 2015; Hazzard''s Ch. 30)'),
