icated for renal protection in patients with diabetes and albuminuria/CKD; a **bisphosphonate or other anti-resorptive** is strongly indicated for osteoporosis prevention in an older patient with a prior fragility fracture (vertebral fracture). (Ref: O''Mahony et al., Age Ageing 2015; Hazzard''s Ch. 30)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old man presents with progressive dizziness, recurrent near-syncope, and cognitive slowing. The patient brings a large paper bag containing **6 active prescription medications** from three different specialists, along with five over-the-counter supplements. Orthostatic vitals show a blood pressure drop of **24 mmHg** upon standing. Which of the following is the correct definition of polypharmacy, and what is the best stepwise strategy for a ''brown-bag review'' and deprescribing?', '["The clinician should instruct the patient to throw away the paper bag and purchase only branded over-the-counter vitamins", "The ''brown-bag review'' is a safety inspection of the patient''s food storage areas to rule out mold contamination", "Polypharmacy is defined as taking more than 20 medications; the best strategy is to immediately stop all medications cold turkey without tapering", "Polypharmacy is typically defined as the concomitant use of \u22655 medications; the best strategy is a systematic review of all active bottles (''brown-bag review''), identifying duplications, screening for drug-drug interactions, assessing indications, and step-by-step deprescribing of non-essential or high-risk drugs", "The presence of polypharmacy indicates that the patient requires more specialist consultations to manage each medication separately"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Polypharmacy** is most commonly defined as the concurrent use of **five or more prescription medications**. It is a major risk factor for adverse drug events, drug-drug interactions, cognitive decline, falls, and mortality. A **''brown-bag review''** is a critical clinical process where the patient brings in ALL actual medication bottles (including OTCs, supplements, and herbals). The clinician performs medication reconciliation, checks for: (1) Therapeutic duplication (taking two drugs in the same class); (2) Prescribing cascades; (3) Indication-prescription mismatch (drugs with no active indication); (4) Potentially inappropriate medications (Beers list). **Deprescribing** is the systematic, supervised process of tapering and discontinuing unnecessary, ineffective, or high-risk medications, which has been shown to improve cognition, reduce falls, and enhance quality of life in older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 30; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
