    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old female presents with progressive dizziness, recurrent near-syncope, and cognitive slowing. The patient brings a large paper bag containing **10 active prescription medications** from three different specialists, along with five over-the-counter supplements. Orthostatic vitals show a blood pressure drop of **24 mmHg** upon standing. Which of the following is the correct definition of polypharmacy, and what is the best stepwise strategy for a ''brown-bag review'' and deprescribing?', '["The presence of polypharmacy indicates that the patient requires more specialist consultations to manage each medication separately", "The ''brown-bag review'' is a safety inspection of the patient''s food storage areas to rule out mold contamination", "Polypharmacy is typically defined as the concomitant use of \u22655 medications; the best strategy is a systematic review of all active bottles (''brown-bag review''), identifying duplications, screening for drug-drug interactions, assessing indications, and step-by-step deprescribing of non-essential or high-risk drugs", "The clinician should instruct the patient to throw away the paper bag and purchase only branded over-the-counter vitamins", "Polypharmacy is defined as taking more than 20 medications; the best strategy is to immediately stop all medications cold turkey without tapering"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Polypharmacy** is most commonly defined as the concurrent use of **five or more prescription medications**. It is a major risk factor for adverse drug events, drug-drug interactions, cognitive decline, falls, and mortality. A **''brown-bag review''** is a critical clinical process where the patient brings in ALL actual medication bottles (including OTCs, supplements, and herbals). The clinician performs medication reconciliation, checks for: (1) Therapeutic duplication (taking two drugs in the same class); (2) Prescribing cascades; (3) Indication-prescription mismatch (drugs with no active indication); (4) Potentially inappropriate medications (Beers list). **Deprescribing** is the systematic, supervised process of tapering and discontinuing unnecessary, ineffective, or high-risk medications, which has been shown to improve cognition, reduce falls, and enhance quality of life in older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 30; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old woman with moderate cognitive decline and severe knee osteoarthritis is brought to the clinic for new-onset urinary incontinence over the past 2 weeks. The patient has been having frequent accidents at home and is highly distressed. The clinician recalls the **DIAPPERS mnemonic** for transient, reversible causes of urinary incontinence in older adults. Urinalysis shows pyuria and bacteriuria, and the patient was recently started on a strong first-generation antihistamine for allergies. Which of the following represents the most appropriate initial management?', '["Recommend immediate surgical bladder neck suspension as first-line therapy for all new-onset incontinence", "Treat the symptomatic urinary tract infection (Infection), discontinue the first-generation antihistamine (Pharmaceuticals), and optimize pain management for knee arthritis to improve mobility (Restricted mobility)", "Reassure the family that new-onset incontinence is an expected and irreversible progression of dementia", "Place an indwelling Foley catheter permanently to manage the incontinence and avoid diagnostic testing", "Prescribe oxybutynin immediately to suppress detrusor contractions, while continuing the antihistamine"]'::jsonb, 1, 'New-onset urinary incontinence in older adults should always prompt an evaluation for **transient, reversible causes** before considering chronic therapies or surgery. The **DIAPPERS mnemonic** covers these causes: (D) **Delirium**; (I) **Infection** (symptomatic UTI); (A) **Atrophic vaginitis/urethritis**; (P) **Pharmaceuticals** (e.g., highly anticholinergic antihistamines causing retention/overflow, or diuretics causing urgency); (P) **Psychological** (severe depression); (E) **Excess urine output** (hyperglycemia, hypercalcemia); (R) **Restricted mobility** (severe arthritis preventing timely access to the toilet); (S) **Stool impaction** (compressing the bladder neck). In this patient, addressing three transient factors is first-line: treating the UTI, stopping the high-risk antihistamine, and managing knee arthritis pain to restore mobility. Adding oxybutynin (a highly anticholinergic Beers drug) would worsen retention and cognition. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 78)'),
